## Restricted mean survival time (RMST) comparisons

While the log-rank test tells you whether groups differ, it does not tell you by how much. The
restricted mean survival time (RMST) is an intuitive effect measure: the expected survival time over
a fixed time window `tau`, computed as the area under the survival curve. For example, 1-year RMST
is the average survival time during the first year. Unlike hazard ratios, RMST is on the survival
time scale, making it easy to interpret and explain to non-technical audiences.

### Two-group RMST comparison

Compare RMST between two groups with confidence intervals and hypothesis tests using `rmst_test()`.
Here we compare 1-year RMST between the two sexes in the `lung` dataset.

::: {#5315f6b1 .cell execution_count=9}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Load data and build the response
lung = gw.load_dataset("lung", backend="polars")
y = gw.Surv.right(lung["time"], event=(lung["status"] == 2))

# Compare 1-year RMST between groups
result = gw.rmst_test(y, tau=365, group=lung["sex"])

# Display the RMST per group, their difference, and the p-value
result
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=9}
```
RMSTResult(method='RMST difference (tau=365)', estimate=-55.9703, se=14.9581, 95% CI=[-85.2877, -26.6529], p_value=0.0001827)
```
:::
:::


The result shows the RMST for each group, their difference, standard error, 95% confidence interval,
and a two-sided p-value testing equality. A positive difference means group 1 has higher RMST
(longer average survival).

You can extract specific components:

::: {#c865cdf1 .cell execution_count=10}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Extract the key effect-size components from the result object
print(f"RMST difference: {result.estimate:.1f} days")
print(f"95% CI: [{result.lower_ci:.1f}, {result.upper_ci:.1f}]")
print(f"P-value: {result.p_value:.4f}")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}
```
RMST difference: -56.0 days
95% CI: [-85.3, -26.7]
P-value: 0.0002
```
:::
:::


### Alternative estimands

By default, `rmst_test()` returns the RMST difference. You can also compute the ratio or percentage
difference using the `estimand` parameter.

**Ratio of RMSTs** (group 1 / group 2):

::: {#e51234b4 .cell execution_count=11}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Ratio estimand: group 1 RMST divided by group 2 RMST
result_ratio = gw.rmst_test(y, tau=365, group=lung["sex"], estimand="ratio")

# A value < 1 means group 1 has shorter average survival
result_ratio.estimate
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=11}
```
0.8118425788462916
```
:::
:::


A ratio of `0.8` means group 1 has 80% of the RMST of group 2 (20% shorter average survival).

**Percentage difference** ((group 1 - group 2) / group 2 × 100):

::: {#99210ab2 .cell execution_count=12}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Percentage-difference estimand: (group 1 - group 2) / group 2 × 100
result_pct = gw.rmst_test(y, tau=365, group=lung["sex"], estimand="percentage_difference")

# Negative means group 1 has shorter average survival as a percentage
result_pct.estimate
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=12}
```
-18.81574211537084
```
:::
:::


All three estimands use the delta method to construct standard errors and confidence intervals, and
support flexible confidence levels via the `conf_level=` parameter.

### Pairwise RMST comparisons

When you have three or more groups, use `pairwise_rmst_test()` to compare all pairs with
multiple-comparison adjustment. Here we test which cell types in the `veteran` dataset differ in
1-year RMST.

::: {#3595c605 .cell execution_count=13}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Load the veteran dataset and build the response
vet = gw.load_dataset("veteran", backend="polars")
y_vet = gw.Surv.right(vet["time"], event=vet["status"])

# Pairwise RMST differences with Holm adjustment
pairs = gw.pairwise_rmst_test(y_vet, tau=365, group=vet["celltype"])

# Display the full pairwise comparison table
pairs
```

